import{_ as a,o as t,c as i,a9 as n}from"./chunks/framework.asyjyako.js";const h=JSON.parse('{"title":"Contributing","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"contributing/index.md","filePath":"contributing/index.md","lastUpdated":null}'),o={name:"contributing/index.md"};function s(r,e,l,d,c,p){return t(),i("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n(`<h1 id="Contributing" tabindex="-1">Contributing <a class="header-anchor" href="#Contributing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Contributing {#Contributing}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Fix code, improve a page or contribute a reproducible experiment. Start with a focused <a href="https://github.com/Mirage-Interactive-Fr/PerfChecker.jl/issues" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">issue</a> or pull request explaining the problem, expected behavior and checks you actually ran.</p><h2 id="Code-and-bug-reports" tabindex="-1">Code and bug reports <a class="header-anchor" href="#Code-and-bug-reports" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Code and bug reports {#Code-and-bug-reports}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Include the Julia/PerfChecker versions, operating system, selected interface or collector, a minimal input and the complete error. For a measurement problem, retain the workload, oracle, units, sample settings and relevant bundle provenance. Remove secrets before sharing reports.</p><p>Keep changes focused and follow the repository&#39;s SciML JuliaFormatter style. Add a regression test for changed behavior; update its docstring and runnable example. Test the affected package in its own environment. A companion or native-tool result does not qualify every interface or platform. Say which checks could not run.</p><p>The project follows <a href="https://github.com/SciML/ColPrac" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">ColPrac</a>&#39;s constructive review and focused contribution principles. Prefer review by another maintainer. A sole maintainer may self-review after recording the change and validation; identify that exception explicitly. Registration and publication remain separate maintainer actions after review.</p><h2 id="Documentation" tabindex="-1">Documentation <a class="header-anchor" href="#Documentation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Documentation {#Documentation}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Explain the operation, show runnable code, then describe the real output and its limits. Put saved plots beside the comparison they explain; keep collection out of documentation builds. Use an existing example&#39;s data when illustrating behavior, and distinguish unavailable tools from successful measurements.</p><details class="details custom-block"><summary>Build and inspect the documentation locally</summary><p>Use the checkout&#39;s supported Julia version and Node.js <strong>22.12 or newer</strong>. The website and each companion API have separate projects. First follow the repository&#39;s <a href="https://github.com/Mirage-Interactive-Fr/PerfChecker.jl/blob/2cf431eb5a84f05e0af1098b7f6e5db84d479288/website/optional-api/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">optional API preparation and rendering instructions</a>. They prepare all six isolated environments, render real docstrings and produce receipts for the checkout&#39;s exact revision. <code>website/make.jl</code> rejects absent, stale or mixed exports.</p><p>Then prepare the website project and build once:</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --project=website</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -e</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;using Pkg; cd(&quot;website&quot;) do; Pkg.develop(path=&quot;..&quot;); Pkg.instantiate(); end&#39;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --project=website</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> website/make.jl</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">node</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> website/preview.mjs</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Open <code>http://127.0.0.1:8870/</code>. The completed site is <code>website/build/site</code>; <code>make.jl</code> already runs VitePress. The local working-tree procedure in the API README is needed when previewing edited package sources. Building does not publish.</p><p>Inspect changed pages, links, search, mobile layout and downloadable examples. The build checks docstrings and references; a successful build still needs a content review.</p></details><h2 id="Contribute-an-example" tabindex="-1">Contribute an example <a class="header-anchor" href="#Contribute-an-example" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Contribute an example {#Contribute-an-example}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Start with a small lifecycle: prepare deterministic input, perform one meaningful operation and verify it independently. Keep the oracle and preparation outside timing. Test correctness before collecting measurements; start with a small explicit selection.</p><p>Include package versions or immutable revisions, dependency manifests, fixture code and seed/frozen inputs, Julia/OS/machine/thread settings, collector and units, sample count and raw observations or a bundle. Explain the baseline and any repeated confirmation. Report failed and unavailable checks alongside successful ones.</p><p>Generate plots from those saved observations. A version comparison needs at least two recorded targets; latest-release profiles explain one workload and must keep their fixture size visible. Supply a downloadable Pluto notebook that prepares its dependencies and source without a repository checkout, opens in Pluto&#39;s browser UI and starts workers only after an explicit action.</p><p>Keep large recordings or bundles at a durable download location. A code or notebook change belongs in the same focused pull request as its explanation and validation. <a href="./../tutorials/bibliography">Bibliography</a>, <a href="./../real-packages/datastructures">DataStructures</a> and <a href="./../real-packages/oxygen">Oxygen</a> show complete experiments.</p><h2 id="Read-qualification-results" tabindex="-1">Read qualification results <a class="header-anchor" href="#Read-qualification-results" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Read qualification results {#Read-qualification-results}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Core, companions and external tools are tested in separate environments. Inspect each selected lane&#39;s exact source, runtime, environment and verdict; missing or failed lanes cannot be filled from another campaign. Routine targeted tests do not establish a complete release collection, and a documentation build does not qualify the packages it describes.</p><p>CI retains source-only LCOV artifacts, line metrics and source/environment provenance for Core and all six companions, with separate flags: <code>core</code>, <code>linuxperf</code>, <code>likwid</code>, <code>makie</code>, <code>web</code>, <code>pluto</code> and <code>tachikoma</code>. Measurement workers explicitly disable coverage and allocation instrumentation. External uploads are opt-in: a maintainer must activate the repository in Codecov, authorize its GitHub integration and set repository variable <code>PERFCHECKER_CODECOV_UPLOAD=true</code>; the uploader then authenticates through GitHub OIDC and reports upload errors as failures. Disabled uploads leave the artifacts available without claiming publication. Add a coverage badge only after the first successful upload is visible for the tested revision.</p><p><a href="./../tools/pkgeval">PkgEval</a> tests an installed package in a Linux sandbox. Match its source kind, registered tree or candidate revision and Julia configuration before interpreting the result. It is a compatibility test rather than a benchmark comparison.</p>`,18)])])}const b=a(o,[["render",s]]);export{h as __pageData,b as default};
